## DESCRIPTION
## Find a linear regresion line through record 100m swimming times.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Lines and Linear Functions')
## DBchapter('')
## DBsection('')
## KEYWORDS('lines, linear functions')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')
## Date('7/10/2022')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',         'PGML.pl',
    'niceTables.pl',         'parserAssignment.pl',
    'PGstatisticsmacros.pl', 'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Numeric");
parser::Assignment->Allow;
parser::Assignment->Function("T");

@swim_times = (
    [ 1970, 51.94 ],
    [ 1975, 50.59 ],
    [ 1980, 49.44 ],
    [ 1985, 48.95 ],
    [ 1990, 48.42 ],
    [ 1995, 48.21 ],
    [ 2000, 47.84 ]
);

($m, $b) = linear_regression(
    [ map { $_->[0] - 1970 } @swim_times ],
    [ map { $_->[1] } @swim_times ]
);
$line = Formula("T(x) = $m x + $b");

$rand_time = random(45.5, 47, 0.1);
$year1     = Round(1970 + ($rand_time - $b) / $m);

BEGIN_PGML
The Men's 100m freestyle (swimming) world records in given years are in the table below:

[#
    [.year.][.time (sec).]*{headerrow => 1}
    [. .]*{rows => ~~@swim_times}
#]{horizontalrules => 1,
    padding         => [ 0.25, 0.25 ],
    align           => '|c|c|'}


a) Let [`x`] be the number of years since 1970.  Find a linear model for [`T(x)`], the time of the world record that best fits this data. Write your answer in the form [`T(x) = m x + b`].  ( Use software to do this and include the slope and y-intercept to at least 4 decimal places.)
  
    [_]{$line}{30}

b) Use this linear model to predict the world record in the 2008 Olympics.  Give your answer to hundredth's of a second.

    [_]{Formula("$m x + $b")->eval(x=> 38)}{10} 

    For your curiosity, the world record that was achieved in the 2008 Olympics was 47.05 seconds.  (Note: this is not what the linear model predicts.) 

c) Use the linear model to estimate the year in which the record will be [$rand_time]. 

    Year = [_]{$year1}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Use software to find the least square/regression line for the data.  You should get get [`m=[@Round($m,5)@]`] and [`b = [@ Round($b,5)@]`], so the linear model should be [``` [$line] ```]

b) Using the model, we plug in 38, which is the number of years since 1970 to get
[``` T(38) = [@Round($m,5)@]\cdot 38 + [@ Round($b,5)@] = [@ Round(38*$m+$b, 5) @] ```]

c) For this we let [`T(x) = [$rand_time]`] and plug into the model and solve for [`x`].

[``` 
\begin{aligned}
[$rand_time] & = [@Round($m,5)@]x + [@ Round($b,5)@] \\
[$rand_time] - [@ Round($b,5)@] & = [@Round($m,5)@] x \\
\frac{[@ Round($rand_time -$b,5)@]}{[@ Round($m,5) @]} & = x \\
[@ Round(($rand_time-$b)/$m,5) @] = x 
\end{aligned}
```]
and the first rounding to the nearest whole number and adding 1970 to get the year results in [$year1].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
